From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 09:09:20 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] README: document the decoder

The decoder reports its resources against a pixel rate of its own, takes a
pixel format parameter, pools the output of a client that decodes to byte
buffers and hands a picture to the host for a GPU consumer. None of that
was written down, and a product that enables the decoder sets a property
the README did not name.
---
 README.md | 69 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 61 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index 1012aae38c1f05c7b4b0109b8707c5831aab8b46..3e2c5a35e3dbcded737beb14cb14b4fb63a5ad0e 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -381,17 +381,23 @@ resources it needs, so that Android can tell what is left
 - Codec sessions (*C2PlatformConfig::VENDOR_START*): the capacity is the sum of
 the concurrent instance bounds of the codecs the store offers, and every
 instance needs one.
-- Pixel rate (*VENDOR_START* + 1): the capacity is
+- Encoder pixel rate (*VENDOR_START* + 1): the capacity is
 *ro.vendor.v4l2_codec2.video_pixel_rate*. An instance needs the width times the
 height times the frame rate (the operating rate, if it is set) of its stream
 when it runs in real time, and nothing when it does not.
-
-An encoder reports a change of its need with its next output. A start that
-would take the pixel rate of all instances past the capacity fails with
-*C2_NO_MEMORY*, which Android raises as *ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_RESOURCE*; the first
-instance is always admitted, as the device serves one of whatever size it
-supports. A decoder takes part by reporting *videoResourcesNeeded()* and
-claiming its pixel rate from *VideoPixelRateBudget* (*VideoResources.h*).
+- Decoder pixel rate (*VENDOR_START* + 2): the same, counted against
+*ro.vendor.v4l2_codec2.video_decode_pixel_rate* for the decoders, because the
+device sustains a different rate for decoding than for encoding. Omit a pixel
+rate property to report sessions only; the store reports a pixel rate only for
+a direction it offers.
+
+A component reports a change of its need with its next output. A start that
+would take the pixel rate of all instances of its direction past the capacity
+fails with *C2_NO_MEMORY*, which Android raises as
+*ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_RESOURCE*; the first instance is always admitted, as the
+device serves one of whatever size it supports. A component takes part by
+reporting *videoResourcesNeeded()* and claiming its pixel rate from
+*VideoPixelRateBudget* or *VideoDecodePixelRateBudget* (*VideoResources.h*).
 
 ### Supported Input Pixel Formats:
 
@@ -415,4 +421,51 @@ PPS NAL units are prepended to IDR frames by enabling the
 support this control the encoder will manually cache and prepend SPS and PPS NAL
 units.
 
+## V4L2 Decoder
+
+### Supported Codecs
+
+The decoders are selected by name: *c2.v4l2.avc.decoder* for H.264 and
+*c2.v4l2.hevc.decoder* for HEVC, enabled by
+*ro.vendor.v4l2_codec2.decoder.supported.h264* and *.hevc* (VP8 and VP9 follow
+the same pattern). The profiles and levels come from the device. The device
+lists the HEVC levels by their Main tier, so the decoder also offers the High
+tier of every level from 4 up, which a stream of that tier needs. Its H.264
+profiles and levels stop at High and level 5.1, so the decoder also offers
+Constrained High, a subset of High, and level 5.2, which differs from 5.1 by the
+macroblock rate and not by the picture size.
+
+### Output Pixel Formats and Buffers
+
+The component takes *C2_PARAMKEY_PIXEL_FORMAT* on its output: flexible YUV
+4:2:0 always, P010 when the device lists a 10-bit profile (HEVC Main 10, VP9
+profile 2), and *IMPLEMENTATION_DEFINED*, which the framework sets when the
+client renders to a Surface. A client that asks for flexible 4:2:0 gets 8-bit
+samples from a 10-bit stream when the device converts them, and a client that
+asks for P010 gets P010. A client that renders to a Surface gets P010 from a
+10-bit stream when the usage of the Surface's consumer
+(*C2_PARAMKEY_OUTPUT_STREAM_USAGE*) names a video encoder, which then has all 10
+bits, and 8-bit samples otherwise, for a display that cannot show P010.
+
+A client that decodes to byte buffers configures the basic graphic block pool,
+whose blocks carry no pool data for the frame pool to identify them by; the
+component then creates a pool of its own from the gralloc allocator. The
+device writes the guest pages of a decoded picture, so a block that a
+consumer reads through the GPU is mapped for writing and unmapped before it is
+handed on, which transfers it to the host copy; every block of a decoder is
+allocated with CPU write usage for that.
+
+A client that detaches the output surface or sets another one hands the frame
+pool buffers the decoder has not met. The pool counts the buffers whose frames
+are still in use against the number of V4L2 buffers, and the decoder lets a free
+V4L2 buffer stand for a block it has not met, since the picture buffer is passed
+again with every queue operation.
+
+### Colour
+
+The component reads the colour aspects of a stream from its SPS VUI for every
+coded video sequence. A stream that states a range (*video_full_range_flag*)
+without a colour description reports that range, with the colour description
+left unspecified.
+
